WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.880
Proposition thirty-eight asks California to borrow eight point four

2
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.980
billion dollars for immunology and immunotherapy research.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.840
Immunology studies the immune system; immunotherapy aims to use

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.440
it to fight disease.

5
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.880
This funds research, not a guaranteed new treatment.

6
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:26.720
Universities, businesses, and governments already fund medical research.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.880
Many projects never become approved treatments.

8
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.500
A research bond supplies money now, while the state

9
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.220
repays the borrowing with interest over time.

10
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.040
Imagine a scientist studying immune cells.

11
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.640
A promising laboratory result still needs testing for safety

12
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.180
and effectiveness.

13
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.220
Some ideas fail, and successful ones can take years.

14
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.560
Funding more work can create opportunities without letting anyone

15
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.600
honestly promise when a patient will benefit.

16
00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.660
Research teams need facilities, people, and sustained funding.

17
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.480
About half the money would go to one qualifying

18
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.360
research institute selected by the state.

19
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.820
The other half would support competitive grants through a

20
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.660
council of research institutions.

21
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.340
Reporting and financial audits are required.

22
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.600
At least four point two billion dollars must support

23
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.880
research on cancer, heart disease, and Alzheimer’s disease.

24
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.980
Treatments developed with the funding generally must be offered

25
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.200
at a twenty percent discount in California.

26
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.240
A discount matters only if a treatment reaches patients.

27
00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.640
The analyst estimates five hundred to six hundred million

28
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.040
dollars a year in General Fund repayments for about

29
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.020
twenty years.

30
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.880
Revenue from discoveries could offset costs, but its amount

31
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.420
and timing are uncertain and could take decades.

32
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.500
A voter might see long-term public research as worth

33
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.520
supporting even when returns are uncertain.

34
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.520
Discoveries could improve lives and build scientific capacity.

35
00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.960
They may accept borrowing because waiting for annual funding

36
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.900
also has an opportunity cost.

37
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.580
You might ask whether this is the best research

38
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.400
investment.

39
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.920
Another voter could object to the debt, the concentration

40
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.500
of funding in one institute, or committing money before

41
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.280
knowing results.

42
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.280
They might favor broader competition or other health priorities.

43
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.320
The possibility of royalties should not be treated as

44
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.700
a promise that the bond pays for itself.

45
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.420
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 55

46
00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:33.820
percent said yes, 42 percent no, and 3 percent

47
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:34.380
were unsure.

48
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.840
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

49
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.840
Yes authorizes this research borrowing; no rejects this bond

50
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.420
without ending existing medical research.

51
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.800
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

52
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.340
and official voter guide.

53
00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.260
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
